+## DeepSeek's tools search
+
+A keyed DeepSeek provider — by its `deepseek` preset, or its Chat host
+`api.deepseek.com` for one added by hand — searches through its tools API
+rather than a model
+([`search_deepseek.go`](../../internal/gateway/search_deepseek.go)):
+`deepSeekSearch` POSTs the provider's Chat base URL with `/tools/search` a
+`{"query","count":10,"need_content":true}` with the key as a Bearer and
+magpie's search User-Agent, and maps the reply's `results` like the search
+APIs' are: a page without a URL is dropped, the title falls back to the
+URL, the text comes from `content`, then `summary`, then `snippet`,
+clipped to 1500 bytes at a rune, and `date` becomes the page's age;
+`site_name`, `site_icon`, `authority` and `rerank_score` are ignored and
+DeepSeek's order is kept. Up to 16 MiB of the reply is read, above the
+search APIs' 4 MiB, because ten results with content can be past it; a
+reply cut by the cap is no JSON any more and fails as an unexpected reply.
+
+The service is one of `serviceSearchers`
+([`search_service.go`](../../internal/gateway/search_service.go)), so it
+behaves as a Kimi Code plan's does: Settings › Web search offers it as a
+service choice with no model, naming it searches with it
+(`chosenSearcher` answers no model, `searcherSpent` passes), and its rank
+is the manual-only one. The exclusion lives where the rank is computed:
+`searchRank` returns -1 for a provider with a keyed service, so
+`autoSearcher` never picks it and the model-based DeepSeek searcher entry
+is gone — a setup whose only searcher is DeepSeek stops searching until
+the service is named. `searchesItself` and the host lists keep their
+answer, so an Anthropic- or Responses-protocol request to a DeepSeek model
+still passes its `web_search` tool through for DeepSeek to run
+(`TestDeepSeekPassThroughUnchanged`).
+
+The provider's keys are asked in turn (`KeysOn`), one request per key with
+no retry: a key the API refuses — 401, 402, 403, 429, 432, 433 — rests for the
+service alone (`serviceRest`: the provider's id scoped to its tools
+service, so a refusal of the tools API benches no model request — a page
+DeepSeek won't read is no word on the key's chats), and the next key on is
+asked; anything else (a 400's message, 408, 5xx, no answer in time) comes
+back as the failure it is, no key rested, and the search falls through
+like any searcher's. An empty result set is found nothing. A call that
+answers with pages records one usage row — kind `web_search_api`, the
+provider and the key used, no tokens, and $0.00075 on the row's own-cost
+field, counted against the calling gateway key's limit; a failure or an
+empty result records nothing. A DeepSeek model's own searches never come
+here unnamed: a service billed per call is never an own-searcher
+(`searchesOwn`), so they run the normal chain
+(`TestDeepSeekOwnModelSearchIsNotAutoBilled`). Covered by
+`TestDeepSeekServiceSearches`, `TestDeepSeekServiceMapsResults`,
+`TestDeepSeekServiceManualOnly`, `TestDeepSeekSearchFailures`,
+`TestDeepSeekSearchUsageRow` and `TestDeepSeekPassThroughUnchanged` in
+[`search_deepseek_test.go`](../../internal/gateway/search_deepseek_test.go).
+
+## A Remote magpie's web search
+
+A keyed Remote magpie searches through the web-search MCP server of its
+own ([`search_pass.go`](../../internal/gateway/search_pass.go)):
+`remoteMagpieSearch` POSTs the remote's base — its Chat base carries
+`/v1`, which is stripped — with `SearchMCPPath` one JSON-RPC `tools/call`
+for `web_search` of the masked query, with the provider's key as the
+gateway key and magpie's User-Agent. The remote's whole chain — its
+searcher, its Search APIs, its services — answers, and its answer text is
+the local search result whole, with no pages: no Sources list is added.
+
+Every pass-through carries the searching mark (`SearchingHeader`), so the
+remote runs no searcher of its own for it. A search another magpie sent
+here (`searchingFrom`, recorded as `searchFromMagpie`) is answered, but
+its named remote service is not asked — `remoteMagpieSearch` refuses it —
+so two magpies that name each other's services cannot loop. A transport
+failure, a timeout, an HTTP error or a remote tool error is the failure it
+is — a tool error surfaces the text the remote wrote — and the search
+falls through like any searcher's; nothing is retried.
+
+The service joins `serviceSearchers` like the others and is offered as a
+manual-only choice with no model — but unlike the other keyed services, a
+remote's natively-searching models keep their place beside it:
+`searchRank` skips the keyed-service exclusion for a remote, so a remote
+with native models keeps its rank and `Searchers` lists it twice, its web
+search and its models. Naming it bare searches through its service;
+naming it with a model searches by the model, marked, as today.
+`searchesOwn` never claims a remote's service, so its own models' searches
+are unchanged. A successful call records a zero-cost row — kind
+`web_search_api`, the remote as the provider with its key, no tokens, the
+calling gateway key and agent, and no own cost (the spend is the remote's,
+on its own usage); a failure records nothing. Covered by
+`TestRemoteMagpieServiceSearches`, `TestRemoteMagpieSearchMarksCall`,
+`TestRemoteMagpieSearchFailures`, `TestRemoteMagpieSearchRow` and
